```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: red
REAL_USE_CASE: No production multi-tool agent team needs a pre-call schema-overlap predictor. In real deployments (BFCL-multiple, ToolBench, Gorilla-API style), engineers already have the agent's own tool-choice logprob/confidence from the model forward pass — the standard, essentially free signal. Generic prompt-difficulty heuristics (length, perplexity) are even cheaper and do not require toolset analysis. The hypothetical user who cannot run the model at all but wants to predict error-prone toolsets does not exist in practice: you must run the model to get a tool choice, and the logprob is available at that point.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only artifact. L0 shows schema_max AUC 0.48–0.56, marginal at K=3 (~0.55) and at/below chance at K>=6 (~0.50) — the realistic regime for production toolsets. Generic difficulty decisively beats schema in all 12 cells (delta CI entirely <0; e.g., K=6,rho=0.5,σ=1.5: −0.202 [−0.230,−0.174]; difficulty AUC 0.59–0.69). Adding schema to agent logprob yields delta-AUC ≤0 in 11/12 cells and strictly hurts in 7/12 (e.g., K=12,rho=0.5,σ=0.5: −0.043 [−0.059,−0.028]; K=6,rho=0,σ=0.5: −0.040 [−0.057,−0.024]). Oracle gap ~0.18–0.23 AUC confirms lexical schema is a lossy readout of latent confusability. Even with neural embeddings (L1), the agent's logprob already integrates confusability+difficulty, so schema cannot add beyond it — the load-bearing bar fails.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [1] Strictly dominated by existing signals: agent self-logprob integrates both confusability and difficulty and dominates the noisy single-factor schema proxy (delta≤0 in 11/12, hurts in 7/12). Generic difficulty beats schema in every cell with CI <0. [2] Fails at realistic scale: K>=6 yields AUC ~0.50, at/below chance; production agents routinely use dozens of tools. [3] No pre-call advantage: the "cheap, no rollout" value prop is moot because production systems already pay for the forward pass and get logprobs for free. [4] Engineering overhead for negative value: requires embedding maintenance and pairwise similarity compute per call, for a signal that degrades performance. [5] Relabeling, not a fresh axis: schema geometry is a noisier re-expression of information already carried better by difficulty and logprob — same failure mode as CLAIM-0015 (quant-router) and CLAIM-0037 (failure-attribution).
RATIONALE: L0 RESULTS (EXP-0035) show (A) schema AUC 0.48–0.56, at/below chance for K>=6, oracle gap ~0.18–0.23 AUC (cells.csv); (B) vs generic difficulty FAILS in ALL 12 cells, delta CI entirely <0 (bootstrap.csv); ADDS BEYOND SELF-LOGPROB: NO, delta≤0 in 11/12 cells, hurts in 7/12 (bootstrap.csv). Mechanism: "lexical overlap recovers little latent confusability; the agent's self-logprob already integrates confusability+difficulty so it strictly dominates the noisy single-factor schema proxy" (RESULTS.md). ENV CAVEAT acknowledges lexical proxy, but "the load-bearing kill is 'adds beyond self-logprob', and the agent's logprob already integrates everything, so a better schema feature is unlikely to add beyond it" (PACKET). Prior art: tool retrieval (ToolLLM, Gorilla, ToolBench) and tool-selection-error literature exist; intended novelty was pre-call schema as error predictor beyond logprob — L0 falsifies this incremental claim (RESULTS.md "Prior-art caveat"). The negative result is clean and actionable: guidance to practitioners is "use agent logprob or generic difficulty; do not waste engineering on pre-call schema geometry" — but the product itself has no real use case.
```
